WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.100
This initiative goes further than any other
initiative anywhere in the world when it comes

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.980
to marijuana legalization.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:15.680
Hi, I'm Zach Weissmueller for Reason TV, here
with Lynne Lyman, California state director

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:21.300
for the Drug Policy Alliance and proponent
of Prop 64, which would legalize commercial

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.020
marijuana sales in California.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:27.840
So, if you have anything you want to know
about marijuana legalization, start posting

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.250
in the comments section of this video right
now.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:36.380
So, we've seen commercial legalization in
several states now: Colorado, Washington,

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:41.950
Oregon, Alaska... something resembling it
in Washington, D.C., although because that's

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:44.719
a federal district, it hasn't really been
implemented.

00:00:44.719 --> 00:00:49.550
What does it mean for the country if California
jumps on that bandwagon and legalizes it?

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:54.559
This initiative goes further than any other
initiative anywhere in the world when it comes

00:00:54.559 --> 00:00:55.750
to marijuana legalization.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:01:01.289
You know, we're really setting a new floor
for what marijuana legalization should include.

00:01:01.289 --> 00:01:04.580
This is not just about people being able to
smoke their weed.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.749
This not about people being able to make their
money.

00:01:07.749 --> 00:01:13.380
You know, those things will happen in the
process, but this is about rectifying decades

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:18.840
of a failed war on drugs that has really been
driven by marijuana offenses if you look at

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.460
drug arrests.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:26.649
And so, to address that, we've taken some
steps in this initiative that we see as reparative

00:01:26.649 --> 00:01:27.649
justice.

00:01:27.649 --> 00:01:31.340
The second thing that makes this significant
for the country is the size of California.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:37.939
We will nearly double the number of people
who are living in a jurisdiction that doesn't

00:01:37.939 --> 00:01:40.369
have marijuana prohibition through this initiative.

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:44.179
And so, we see California-- --particularly,
there's four other states as well.

00:01:44.179 --> 00:01:49.439
And if all five of us were to pass marijuana
legalization this year, it could really be

00:01:49.439 --> 00:01:52.259
the tipping point to end prohibition federally.

00:01:52.259 --> 00:01:58.149
Since California last tried this with Prop
19, and failed at it, there's been an inflection

00:01:58.149 --> 00:02:03.979
point in public opinion, where you suddenly
see the majority of people favor marijuana

00:02:03.979 --> 00:02:05.069
legalization.

00:02:05.069 --> 00:02:09.470
In a strange way, the biggest obstacle seems
like it might be complacency.

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:12.610
People see that medical marijuana is legal
here.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:16.319
It's pretty easy to get medical marijuana
for just about anybody.

00:02:16.319 --> 00:02:19.440
So, people think, why bother?

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.730
Marijuana is only legal for white people in
California, practically.

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:26.650
When you look at how the law is being applied.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:32.000
If you are white, and you are 21 or over,
then you can pretty much use marijuana without

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.210
criminal justice involvement.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:37.900
That is not true for African-Americans in
California or even for Latinos.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:42.560
African-Americans are four times more likely
to get stopped for marijuana, Latinos two

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.049
times more likely, despite the fact that white
people use and sell marijuana at slightly

00:02:47.049 --> 00:02:50.180
higher rates than black and Latinos in California.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:57.700
If California were to pass this legalization
of commercial sales of marijuana, what would

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:03.569
you expect would happen in terms of the prison
population, the jail population, do you have

00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:04.569
any idea?

00:03:04.569 --> 00:03:09.299
My guesstimate is it's going to be under 10,000
people who are currently in jail who will

00:03:09.299 --> 00:03:17.060
now be eligible for having their sentence
reduced, much like happened under Prop 47.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:20.060
And this is also applicable retroactively.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:25.280
So to anybody who has a marijuana offense
on their record going back 5, 10, 50 years

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.390
will be eligible to have their record cleared.

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:32.409
And I think that could-- --having gone through
this with Prop 47, where in LA county alone

00:03:32.409 --> 00:03:38.579
we had 800,000 people eligible for Prop 47
record change, I'm guessing that we're going

00:03:38.579 --> 00:03:42.420
to be at half a million, at least, statewide
for the marijuana change.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:46.569
So, hundreds of thousands of Californians
will be able to have their record cleared.

00:03:46.569 --> 00:03:50.989
And I'm thinking it's going to be between
five and ten thousand will be able to petition

00:03:50.989 --> 00:03:52.219
to get released from jail.

00:03:52.219 --> 00:03:57.970
But I think, then, people are maybe thinking,
well, there's not really many people that

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.810
are being punished just for marijuana.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:05.020
But you mentioned that it is kind of the foundation
of the drug war.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:06.739
Could you expound on that a little bit?

00:04:06.739 --> 00:04:11.409
Yeah, well, when you look at mass incarceration
and how we got where we are--for those of

00:04:11.409 --> 00:04:17.280
your viewers who may not know--we have five
percent of the world's population and twenty-five

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.280
percent of the world's prisoners.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:22.750
And what has got us here is the war on drugs
and our drug laws.

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:27.520
So the biggest sort of feeder is drug arrests.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:31.900
About 1.2 to 1.5 million drug-related arrests
a year.

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:35.220
Half of those are marijuana-only offenses.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:41.199
We've seen that law enforcement has really
just not eased up, generating 700-800,000

00:04:41.199 --> 00:04:47.000
arrests in this country every year and continuing
to keep our prisons and our jails, which is

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:51.650
where a lot of these folks are going--even
in other states--overfull.

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:58.801
What has California, in crafting this initiative,
Prop 64, learned from what these other states

00:04:58.801 --> 00:05:00.949
have done right and done wrong?

00:05:00.949 --> 00:05:04.919
The governor recently signed into law medical
marijuana regulation.

00:05:04.919 --> 00:05:11.680
And so, for the most part, Prop 64 just builds
on the bureaucratic structure--call it--there's

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.210
a new bureau: The Bureau of Marijuana Control.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:21.979
It's inside the Department of Consumer Affairs,
kind of like the ABC has always been for liquor

00:05:21.979 --> 00:05:28.810
retailers, they will be for cannabis retailers
and cultivators, all the licenses.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:34.860
So, one of the things we learned was, be intentional,
arrests drop drastically.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:40.860
All marijuana-related arrests drop drastically
after legalization in every jurisdiction.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:47.210
The racial discrimination in that disparity
continues to exist, just at lower numbers.

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:53.630
And so, that was one of the reasons it was
important to protect kids from the criminal

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:55.660
justice system with this initiative.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:59.690
And I'd like to dig a little bit more into
the details of the regulation in a second,

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:02.370
but first we'll turn to our questions.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:05.280
We've got a couple pulled up from Facebook.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:11.569
Josh Kelly asks, "How can we convince long
standing, anti marijuana citizens that marijuana

00:06:11.569 --> 00:06:13.520
is a good thing?"

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:19.490
Well, I don't know if I would ask anybody
who believes that to change their mind and

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:21.720
believe that marijuana is a good thing.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:27.970
What I would ask that person is, "Is marijuana
legalization better than what we have today?"

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:32.980
So, reducing the numbers of people who go
into the criminal justice system for marijuana,

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:37.770
and a product that's safer for consumers,
regulated by public health, and the people

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:40.090
who do that....

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:44.199
Isn't that a better option, even if you don't
like marijuana and you think it's a bad thing.

00:06:44.199 --> 00:06:49.689
Robert Rosales says, "Why can't 18-year-olds
get prescription marijuana when 10-year-olds

00:06:49.689 --> 00:06:51.190
can get oxycontin?"

00:06:51.190 --> 00:06:58.460
Well, actually, Prop 64 does not affect Prop
215, which is the Compassionate Use Act, which

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:01.939
allowed medicinal marijuana for patients.

00:07:01.939 --> 00:07:03.250
We don't touch that.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:05.720
Prop 215 applies to 18 and over.

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:10.300
It's interesting, the politics of medical
marijuana and commercial marijuana.

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:16.980
Because we saw, last time, during Prop 19,
a strange anti-marijuana force arose in Northern

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:22.430
California with the growers: The small, family
farms growing marijuana were afraid they were

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:26.189
going to get crushed by commercialization,
so they actually came out against commercial

00:07:26.189 --> 00:07:27.250
legalization.

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:32.039
What has Prop 64 done to address that and
kind of bring them into the fold?

00:07:32.039 --> 00:07:39.789
One of the big pieces that we try to be responsive
to that concern is a five-year ban on large

00:07:39.789 --> 00:07:40.789
licenses.

00:07:40.789 --> 00:07:45.729
So, for the first five years, no large licenses
will be given out.

00:07:45.729 --> 00:07:48.009
And that's 22,000 square feet or above.

00:07:48.009 --> 00:07:56.319
We also wrote into the licensing regime that
the regulating agency has to consider whether

00:07:56.319 --> 00:08:00.910
handing out this particular license to this
particular business would lead to the creation

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:01.910
of a monopoly.

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:07.599
The licensing is where we get into the areas
of concern for me, from a libertarian perspective.

00:08:07.599 --> 00:08:13.139
There's kind of a self-conscious approach
to approach it like the wine industry or beer,

00:08:13.139 --> 00:08:16.080
where you have the tiered system.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:22.310
So, you know, you can get a license to be
a distributor or a grower or a retail seller,

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:25.530
but it may be impossible to hold all of those
licenses.

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:27.990
There's not what they call, "vertical integration."

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:29.229
Not under Prop 64.

00:08:29.229 --> 00:08:30.229
Oh, ok.

00:08:30.229 --> 00:08:34.760
We put a special license type, license type
number 19, which is called the "micro license."

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:39.450
So for small shops, you can get a license,
fully vertically integrated, 10,000 square

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:40.750
feet or less.

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:46.010
And what's the rationale for this kind of
tiered licensing once they get bigger?

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:51.480
The licensing, for the most part, replicated
what the governor and the legislature passed

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:53.020
for medical last year.

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:54.460
We did add that micro license.

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:55.690
We added the five-year ban.

00:08:55.690 --> 00:09:00.450
So we did a few little improvements around
the edges, to protect the growers and the

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:01.790
small business people.

00:09:01.790 --> 00:09:06.260
And, in terms of personal use, and growing
in your own home, that's treated as a totally

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:07.260
separate thing, right?

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:08.300
Yeah, that's back to personal use.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:13.530
So, you can have up to an ounce of flower,
marijuana flower on you, up to 8 grams of

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:15.390
concentrate, or up to six plants at home.

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:19.961
Let's see, we have another question from Matt
Vest, "What would you say to people who believe

00:09:19.961 --> 00:09:22.970
that cannabis is a 'gateway drug'?"

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:24.090
That it's not.

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:29.140
You just can't find any hard scientific evidence
behind that theory.

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:33.570
What we do see is that people who end up having
problematic drug use at some time later in

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:39.060
their life probably were doing everything
that they could at a really young age.

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:45.110
Meaning, alcohol, tobacco, marijuana, whatever
what is in their family's, you know, medicine

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:46.110
cabinet.

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:52.900
There is no causation between using marijuana
and ending up with problematic drug use.

00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:58.000
Jeff Hamilton asks, "Doesn't over-regulation
of marijuana tend to undercut the gains made

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:03.470
by decriminalization by continuing to make
the the black market attractive for buyers

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:04.690
and sellers?"

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:08.890
It's particularly a challenge in California
that the other states have not had.

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:14.150
Because in California, because of medical
marijuana legalization in 1996, we've had

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:17.520
a 20-year-old marijuana industry.

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:18.630
Most of these states didn't.

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:24.020
So we have to transition our underground market
into a legal market.

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:25.850
The issue of taxation is related to this.

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:32.250
So, if you tax too high, people will stay
in the underground, illicit market.

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:34.210
But you have to tax.

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:39.370
And we've seen that play out in, I believe
in Oregon a little bit, where the taxes were--

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:40.780
--It was Washington.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:41.780
Washington.

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:46.450
Where the taxes on recreational were so high
that everybody stayed in the medical market,

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:48.950
which wasn't being taxed at all.

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:51.080
So they had to adjust.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:53.790
And we may have to adjust as well.

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:59.320
If we just had the one excise tax, which was
at 15 percent under retail, it would be fine.

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:03.270
But counties and cities have the authority
to add additional taxes on.

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:06.730
So if we're getting up to 30, 35 percent tax,
then yeah.

00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:09.490
That's when people are going to stay in the
illicit, underground market.

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:13.360
And we're trying to begin to work with cities
and counties to say, "Don't go crazy.

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:15.550
I know you need money for everything.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:16.600
Don't go crazy.

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:17.600
Start low."

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:21.670
Maria Emmons asks, "Has anyone overdosed on
marijuana?"

00:11:21.670 --> 00:11:25.480
No, but that doesn't mean that marijuana isn't
a--or can't be dangerous.

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:26.480
Right?

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:28.210
It is a psychoactive substance.

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:29.820
No doubt about it.

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:36.120
And consuming too much, consuming it in the
wrong space, consuming it on top of mental

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:41.510
health or physical issues you may have, I
think that what we have to do is provide what

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:46.610
I call "reality-based drug education" to young
people and to adults.

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:48.700
Like, no, it is not going to kill you.

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:49.700
Can it be dangerous?

00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:50.700
Yes.

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:51.700
Here's why.

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:52.960
Don't mix it with alcohol.

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:54.850
Don't take it before you drive.

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:57.830
If you're doing edibles, start small.

00:11:57.830 --> 00:12:00.100
Don't be Maureen Dowd.

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:02.000
Don't be Maureen Dowd!

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.000
Right!

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.730
Keep a journal of how much you took and how
it made you feel.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:08.780
It's going to take sixty to ninety minutes,
so wait your ninety minutes.

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:10.281
So that's what we need to do.

00:12:10.281 --> 00:12:12.440
Not tell people,"Marijuana is going to kill
you!"

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:17.970
We had a question from Cash Foley, who asks,
"Does going further than anywhere else in

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:20.530
the world put the initiative at risk?"

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:22.010
Well... yes!

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:24.950
And this is why people can't be complacent,
and they can't stay at home.

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:28.590
And they can't say, "Ah, it's practically
legal, who needs to vote on it?"

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:30.670
Or, "The line was too long."

00:12:30.670 --> 00:12:34.600
Law enforcement, who is our primary opponents,
are coming on hard.

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:39.150
They have some kind of real big scare tactics
up their sleeve, and if they raise they money,

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:40.890
then it's going to be a tough race.

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:46.090
Some of the tactics I've heard, or, I guess,
the talking points, from that side...

00:12:46.090 --> 00:12:51.040
It's very focused on the "protecting the children"
type of argument.

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:55.840
And you addressed that a little bit at the
beginning in terms of the labeling, and you're

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:58.410
not allowed to sell it near schools.

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:05.060
What about the argument that legalizing something
sends the message to children that it's OK

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:10.570
and therefore more kids are going to experiment
with marijuana before they should?

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:12.790
I don't think it has any merit.

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:17.500
I'm a mother of a 12-year-old and, no, she
cannot touch my alcohol.

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:18.820
She cannot touch my marijuana.

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:23.960
So, I think it's pretty easy for parents to
be clear about something may be legal, but

00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:29.210
it's not safe for children or OK for children
or even acceptable for children.

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:33.110
So I think that that's kind of a dog whistle,
the whole "watch out for the kids" thing,

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:37.610
I think is just, you now, the cops trying
to hide behind the children.

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:42.340
This is an interesting question from Tim Hess,
"Does California have a plan to store the

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:46.740
revenue from weed sales, which has led to
problems in Colorado."

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:52.940
Just so our audience knows what he means,
Colorado took in so much money from commercial

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:59.180
marijuana sales that they had a budget surplus
and they had no law in place with what to

00:13:59.180 --> 00:14:01.200
do with all the extra cash.

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:04.460
So does California have a plan if that should
happen?

00:14:04.460 --> 00:14:09.760
Well, Ithink the California legislature and
the government would be so happy if that happened,

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:10.832
but I don't expect that to happen.

00:14:10.832 --> 00:14:15.920
One of the things we did learn from Colorado
was to be more prescriptive with the revenue

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:16.920
allocation.

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:18.180
So we're very prescriptive.

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:19.310
There's a number of funds.

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:20.900
I can run through them if you want.

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:22.950
Yeah, just give us a taste of where it's going.

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:27.011
So, at full implementation, which will take
a few years, right, there's the revenue is

00:14:27.011 --> 00:14:29.110
expected to be about $1 billion.

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:32.440
That's an estimate from the nonpartisan legislative
analysts office.

00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:38.000
So, of that billion, there's some pieces off
the top: $10 million goes to the UC system

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:40.390
for research on the impact of legalization.

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:45.910
$3 million goes to California Highway Patrol
to do research on driving under the influence.

00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:50.010
The most exciting piece that comes off the
top is this reparative justice piece, which

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:52.420
we call the "community reinvestment fund."

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:57.050
It's $50 million a year that will go into
those communities that were hit the hardest

00:14:57.050 --> 00:15:02.370
by the war on drugs, and provide them with
services, legal services, education, economic

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:05.710
opportunity, financing, support, etc.

00:15:05.710 --> 00:15:08.950
The rest is split in percentages three ways.

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:15.890
60% of the remaining, so we're talking, $500
million potentially, will go to youth drug

00:15:15.890 --> 00:15:19.270
education, treatment, and prevention.

00:15:19.270 --> 00:15:22.490
20% goes to the environment.

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:26.600
And that is for environmental remediation
and restoration.

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:30.430
There's been a lot of damage from illegal
grows, particularly up north.

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:32.480
And then 20% goes back to the local government.

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:36.800
And the local government can pretty much do
what they want with it.

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:40.690
If a local government bans commercial sales,
they will not get their 20%.

00:15:40.690 --> 00:15:47.040
A big outstanding question mark here is, kind
of, what is the federal government going to

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:52.690
do if the largest state in the union legalizes
commercial marijuana?

00:15:52.690 --> 00:15:56.060
We saw under the Obama administration, at
least at the beginning of that administration,

00:15:56.060 --> 00:16:00.450
there was actually a ramp up of raids on medical
marijuana clinics.

00:16:00.450 --> 00:16:03.680
Who knows what a Trump administration would
do on this issue.

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:09.340
And Clinton administration, you know, she's
not even ostensibly as liberal Obama.

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:15.760
So what do you expect might happen at the
federal level if this passes?

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:20.310
Marijuana laws in states are sort of guided
by what's called the "Cole memo," and the

00:16:20.310 --> 00:16:23.820
Cole memo essentially said this--it came out
of Obama's Department of Justice, several

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:28.420
years back--and it said, "We aren't going
to interfere with the states.

00:16:28.420 --> 00:16:33.280
States who want to experiment with marijuana
laws, we're going to stay out, if you follow

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:35.230
the eight or ten guidelines."

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:38.820
So, obviously, in every state where we've
legalized, we have followed the guidelines

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:39.940
to keep the feds out.

00:16:39.940 --> 00:16:47.400
But the Cole memo becomes no longer the sort
of ruling document with a new presidency.

00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:57.720
The other issue is funding for the DEA and
the US Attorneys to prosecute marijuana in

00:16:57.720 --> 00:16:58.720
the states.

00:16:58.720 --> 00:17:04.310
So there was the Rohrbacher amendment, our
congressman from Orange County, Dana Rohrbacher,

00:17:04.310 --> 00:17:10.139
with a number of other California congressmen,
on both sides of the aisle, passed legislation

00:17:10.139 --> 00:17:17.110
a year or two ago that said that the federal
government can't use federal money to interfere

00:17:17.110 --> 00:17:19.110
in a state's marijuana laws.

00:17:19.110 --> 00:17:20.780
So that would actually have to be changed
by Congress.

00:17:20.780 --> 00:17:24.079
The Cole memo could just be changed by the
president.

00:17:24.079 --> 00:17:30.280
But to re-fund the federal government sticking
their hands into our marijuana would take,

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:33.860
I believe, a new vote in Congress.

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:37.120
What do you see in the next 5 or 10 years?

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:41.649
Do you think marijuana legalization is going
to sweep the nation?

00:17:41.649 --> 00:17:47.100
Are we going to start talking about other
drugs being legalized?

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:48.655
What do you expect might happen?

00:17:48.655 --> 00:17:52.919
Well, I do see this as a tipping point for
marijuana, and I do think marijuana legalization

00:17:52.919 --> 00:17:57.710
will sweep the nation and eventually federal
prohibition will be ended.

00:17:57.710 --> 00:18:01.250
I see that on a 5-10 year track.

00:18:01.250 --> 00:18:04.980
But marijuana legalization is really only
just the first step.

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:11.179
So, my organization, the Drug Policy Alliance,
we will be moving towards advancing all drug

00:18:11.179 --> 00:18:12.179
decriminalization.

00:18:12.179 --> 00:18:14.330
So, what we call the "Portugal model."

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:16.559
Where nobody can get arrested for using drugs,
ever.

00:18:16.559 --> 00:18:21.169
I mean, you can still get citations, and you
can be called to do this community service

00:18:21.169 --> 00:18:25.620
or that, but you cannot get arrested for using
drugs in the country of Portugal.

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:26.620
And what have they seen?

00:18:26.620 --> 00:18:29.510
They implemented this in 2001.

00:18:29.510 --> 00:18:34.250
And in those years since, they have seen,
of course incarceration and arrests have gone

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:35.250
down.

00:18:35.250 --> 00:18:36.409
But drug treatment has gone up.

00:18:36.409 --> 00:18:37.779
And staying in drug treatment.

00:18:37.779 --> 00:18:39.559
And youth use has gone down.

00:18:39.559 --> 00:18:41.380
And overdoses have gone way down.

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:47.320
So we really see marijuana as maybe the low-hanging
fruit of this battle.

00:18:47.320 --> 00:18:50.470
And this is all very tied into mass criminalization.

00:18:50.470 --> 00:18:59.090
So, taking away these tools for law enforcement
to overly harass and criminalize people for,

00:18:59.090 --> 00:19:00.919
you know, what they're putting in their bodies.

00:19:00.919 --> 00:19:02.640
Lynne Lyman, thank you for talking to us.

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:03.960
It's good to see you again, Zach.

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:28.559
For Reason TV, I'm Zach Weissmueller.

